  "summary": "GenAI tools for clinical decision-making are moving toward regulatory scrutiny similar to physicians themselves, as FDA officials signal these capabilities will require formal assessment frameworks. Meanwhile, several venture-backed health-tech companies are securing substantial funding—including Iambic's $150M IPO for AI-driven drug discovery, OMass's $310M for rare disease therapeutics, and City Therapeutics' $200M IPO—indicating investor confidence in AI and precision medicine applications. On the care delivery front, telemedicine is demonstrating concrete value in reducing cancer patient admissions and costs, while Epic's new prescription price comparison tool and initiatives around primary care access reflect broader industry efforts to address cost and access barriers. A significant regulatory and investigative focus is emerging around nursing home quality standards and drug pricing integrity, with Massachusetts regulators moving against chains that cut care quality and bipartisan lawmakers urging the FDA to close loopholes enabling counterfeit obesity drugs. The sector is also grappling with structural challenges including Medicare Advantage star rating pressures, the unsustainable economics of cross-subsidization in healthcare systems, and the need for better deployment of existing antibiotics—suggesting that technology alone cannot solve systemic healthcare delivery problems.",
